Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Did You Step Beyond the Chalk?
- Story & Context: In the limestone undercroft of Sankt-Koloman Abbey, Prior Tomas discovered novice Julian hunched over a glowing Doepfer rack at 3 a.m., furiously patching a gate sequencer while breathlessly overblowing a tin piccolo to replicate a dream of collapsing mine shafts. The elder monk lunged to sever the power cable, but Julian shoved the gain into red-line saturation, shouting over the piercing acoustic whistle, "Don't lift your heel until the current breaks." The tape machine captured the violent clash between the wooden flute's shriek and the uncompressed kick drum, yielding a bootlegged cassette that became the foundational track of the entire Basel underground.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Stark overhead monochrome shot of damp monastery flagstones marked with a crude, trembling circle drawn in white builder's chalk. In the center of the ring sits a pair of heavy work boots beside a severed, smoking patch cable; high contrast, gritty concrete textures, minimalist album art aesthetic.

Track 2: Rusty Jackstraws Pierce the Palm
- Story & Context: Recording in an unheated dockside silo in St. Johann, flutist Eleni Vane confessed she had stopped speaking in full sentences after days spent translating her night-sweats into control voltage using discarded tailoring needles as patch pins. Her fingertips left rusted iron smudges across the faceplate of the analog synth as she forced her silver piccolo into shrill, airless multiphonics that mimicked the squeal of failing freight elevators. Leaning her parched mouth into a dynamic microphone between takes, she rasped the track's single vocal motif, "Every strike upon the wire is a debt unpaid." The session ended when her overblown lip split open on the embouchure hole, leaving blood on the microphone grill that the engineers refused to clean.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Extreme macro cinematic photograph of a weathered wooden piccolo resting on a brushed steel Eurorack synth panel, surrounded by scattered, oxidized iron sewing needles and copper wires. Harsh, raking side-light reveals microscopic scratches on the metal and dried liquid droplets; cold, industrial, brutalist album artwork.

Track 3: Attenuated Bell-Tower
- Story & Context: Audio archivist Hannes Meyer entered the evacuated chapel of the Oberndorf cloister to find the floor dusted in fine grey powder from an ornate rose window that had shattered from sustained acoustic resonance. A locked-groove reel-to-reel tape machine was still running unattended through a pair of blown horn speakers, looping a punishing 124 BPM sub-bass kick beneath an ethereal, comb-filtered piccolo drone that documented the prior’s final vision of a silent bell. As Meyer reached to power down the burning motor, the looping voice of an unknown novice cracked through the distortion, intoning, "The stone forgets what the wire remembers." The resulting live room recording was pressed directly to vinyl without EQ corrections, sealing the legend of the vanished brotherhood.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic, low-angle perspective looking up into an empty, brutalist concrete bell tower where the bells have been removed, replaced by dangling cables and a solitary ribbon microphone hung by a wire. Broken glass and fine masonry dust catch harsh, cold beams of exterior sunlight; desaturated tones, austere, haunting, minimalist architecture.

